linemap: #line and debugging

Before every generated line whose .gp line is not the previous line plus one, gocpp writes a
#line N "/abs/path/file.gp" directive. So compiler errors and debuggers (gdb, lldb) point at
.gp lines, and a breakpoint on a .gp line stops at the right place. Your variable names stay
the same in C++, so print s works in the debugger. The lines that have no place in the .gp
source (the C++ main wrapper) point at the generated file itself.

gocpp emit leaves the directives of generated lines out, and gocpp emit -annotate replaces
all of them by the "// ---- file.gp:LINE" headers, which are the same information for
reading. A C++ compile error in generated code is a bug in gocpp: gocpp build says so and
keeps the work folder.

Specification: docs/spec/cpp-mapping.md#line-and-debugging
See also: gocpp explain files, gocpp explain names
Also known as: line, debugging, debug, line-directives

Example (linemap.gp):

    package main

    func add(a, b int) int {
    	return a + b
    }

    func main() {
    	x := add(1, 2)
    	println(x, 10/x)
    }

The C++ that gocpp emit -annotate writes for it:

    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #pragma once
    #include <gocpp/rt.hpp>

    namespace gpkg_main {
    int64_t add(int64_t a, int64_t b);
    void main();

    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/linemap.gp.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/linemap.gp.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    namespace gpkg_main {

    // ---- ./linemap.gp:3: func add(a, b int) int {
    int64_t add(int64_t a, int64_t b) {

    // ---- ./linemap.gp:4: return a + b
        return a + b;

    // ---- ./linemap.gp:5: }
    }

    // ---- ./linemap.gp:7: func main() {
    void main() {

    // ---- ./linemap.gp:8: x := add(1, 2)
        int64_t x = add(1, 2);

    // ---- ./linemap.gp:9: println(x, 10/x)
    // gp[checks] col 15: division of integers: gp::div panics for a zero divisor and gives Go's result
    //     for MIN / -1; these checks stay under -nochecks
        gp::println(x, gp::div(int64_t(10), x));

    // ---- ./linemap.gp:10: }
    }

    } // namespace gpkg_main
